WEBVTT

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.500
Sixteen ships from the Toumai High School
faculty fleet are out of commission?

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.880
Has the Musashi truly mutinied?

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.260
We can't tell from this report.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.470
The Musashi's damage is insignificant.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:17.760
The Harekaze did their best to break away from
their attacks, but in the end, target was lost.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.770
The instructor's ship was state of the art.
So, how?

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.520
It appears all electronics
and guided missiles have malfunctioned.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.190
What about the crew members?

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.980
Their three levels of safety mechanisms
proved very useful.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.650
Zero dead and a few members
with light injuries.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.450
The Musashi's fuel and ammunition?

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.490
At departure, they were fully loaded,

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.960
so they have an estimated 80%
or more left for both fuel and ammo.

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.210
Why were they given so much initially?

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.960
It's difficult to replenish Yamato-class
ammunition on the sea, so...

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:49.420
Principal!

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.680
We lost contact with the Hiei and the Choukai!

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.640
What?!

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.640
Other than the Musashi,
which ships are location unknown?

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:04.640
Hiei, Choukai, Maya, Isuzu, Natori,
Amatsukaze, Isokaze, Tokitsukaze,

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.650
as well as Germany's
training exercise participant,

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.400
Admiral Graf Spee.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.990
That many? Which ships are still in operation?

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.870
Mamiya, Akashi, and Kazahaya,
which are supplying other ships.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.290
And the escort ships Akikaze,
Hamakaze, and Maikaze.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:25.420
Reconnaissance ships are Nagara, Harekaze,
Urakaze, Hagikaze, and Tanikaze. That's all.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.300
Yamashiro, Kaga, Akagi,
Ibuki, and Ikoma are all docked.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.800
No matter how fast we move, we can't
deploy them for at least six months.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.260
It is possible to accelerate that schedule for
destroyers, but at best would be three months.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.810
Can we send any ships
to confront the Musashi?

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.810
Other than the Harekaze, the other ships
are searching for the lost ships,

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.770
so it would be a matter of days.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.500
MUSASHI

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.250
Moka...

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:47.970
<i>Enough already!</i>

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.730
<i>In what world does a captain neglect her ship</i>

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.980
<i>and rushes out whenever there's danger?</i>

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.230
<i>Aren't comrades at sea all family?</i>

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.690
<i>Aren't the crew members
of this ship your family?</i>

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.450
Maybe I'm not qualified to be captain after all.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:07.740
Recovery success.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.250
Are you okay?

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:11.710
Thank you.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.420
You'll catch a cold if you stay soaked like that.
Why don't you go take a bath?

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:18.920
Okay.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.640
I knew our captain would make it back safely.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:29.310
Aye.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.060
The Musashi was awesomzona!

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.190
Ms. Katsuta, what is our current position?

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.810
- I don't knowzona.
- What?

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:37.770
- Zona?
- Zona.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.700
I was trying my best to escape,
so there was no time to discern for locations.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.530
Damage report and area situation check.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.540
I can't see anything ahead of us.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.540
Nothing from port side.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:51.790
Nothing from starboard side either.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.040
Radar is coming up blank.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.750
<i>Communications are down.</i>

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.040
<i>I can't hear anything on sonar either.</i>

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.540
Don't all tell me at once!

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.590
It seems all of our
electronic equipment is wiped out.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:01.800
Is it broken?

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.430
I'm getting a lot of noise, cause unknown.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.640
<i>I can see the stars.</i>

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.640
Astronavigation, quickly!

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.760
Roger!

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.390
Currently Bathing: Engine Department
Don't forget anything; Throw away trash;
Water/hot water left on is forbidden!!

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.440
I have our current position.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.150
Northern latitude of 35°15′29″

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.070
eastern longitude of 136°4′35″!

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.660
Our current position is... what?

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:28.950
Where are we?

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.870
Um... We're...

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.660
Reports are given quickly and accurately!

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.040
We're in the middle of Lake Biwa!

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.290
I see! Lake Biwa, huh?

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.420
Yeah. There's access to it now.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:43.170
No wonder the waves have been so quiet.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.800
Of course we're not in a lake!

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.930
Sorry! We'll check once more!

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.810
I'm soaked in sweat.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.930
Let's get to the bath and freshen up.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.440
Huh? Captain, it's the engineers' bath time.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.070
What kind of example are you setting
when the top doesn't wait its turn?

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.990
Y-yeah.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:11.370
What's this? What's the trouble here?!

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.040
Captain! Oh my-my! You're soaking wet!

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.040
An emergency means
the line goes out the window!

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.330
Now get in there!

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.880
Don't just stand there staring,
you get in here too!

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:29.590
Musashi's attack crippled
Toumai High's instructor ship?

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.100
Is it a student rebellion after all?

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.480
If it is, and the Musashi reaches an urban area,
would they be able to stop it?

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.860
According to the Harekaze's report,
guided missiles didn't work.

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:44.230
Maybe a shower of torpedoes
or bombardment would work?

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.150
The Musashi has the best
and brightest students.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.620
You think you can hit them with
unguided torpedoes fired from out of range?

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.410
This is difficult. But then...

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.750
We can only confront them with
equivalent military strength.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.670
For 18 inches, you need 18 inches, eh?

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.050
But Kure's Yamato and
Maizuru's Shinano are docked.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.090
What about Sasebo's Kii?

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:09.050
No good. They're on a long voyage
on the other side of the world.

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.680
Well, with 14 or 16 inch cannons,
we're just no match.

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.940
The main cannon went from
5 inches to 3.9 inches, right?

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.060
Five inches had its good parts too.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.900
But this is nice.
I want one for my own ship, too.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.070
Warmed up by 60,000 horsepower, this
Harekaze bathtub is the ship's pride and joy!

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.030
- It's tough taking care of it.
- And it often throws tantrums.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:38.870
In the end, we lower the output so there's
no advantage in having a high pressure can.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.750
And the bridge is always
telling us to go at full speed.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:46.210
So Captain, what's happening up-top?

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:46.960
Up-top?

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.720
We stokers have no idea
what's going on outside.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.970
This is our only chance to catch up.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.480
Um, the Toumai High School instructor ship
battled with the Musashi...

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.770
The Musashi? From our school?!

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.360
Toumai High? The one with the submarine?

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.770
If it's an instructor ship,
it's gotta be state of the art.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.690
Our teacher shot at us, too...

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.690
Up-top sounds like a pain.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.700
We stokers can't go anywhere.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.150
Was it the same situation?

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.870
We wanna go somewhere on the skipper, too.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.410
I don't know.
That's why I wanted to stop them...

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:17.200
It's nice when a place runs without
the top person being there.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:17.200
For being the captain, she left us again.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.660
You can't stop something like that
with just one skipper.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.660
If our chief engineer left, we'd be in trouble.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.000
I wish we could go to a cool,
refreshing place some time.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.250
Isn't there another person
who's more qualified to be a captain?

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.170
so sweat won't collect under my breasts.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.170
Kuro!

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:30.050
Since way back, people always said,
"A portable shrine should be light and stupid!"

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.550
Yep, yep.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:38.430
Bow side, frame number 135.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.640
20 cm x 50 cm breach near waterline.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.600
Slight leak of fuel oil confirmed.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:48.110
Wow, that's some major damage.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:51.740
That felt great.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.110
That was a great bath.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.240
You're worried about the Musashi, huh?

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:57.240
Yeah.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.700
Is the captain of the Musashi your friend?

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:02.870
Since we were kids.

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:06.170
I wonder what's happening with the Musashi.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.290
How can they get helped?

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:13.430
Before worrying about other people,
you should take care of your own ship!

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:25.270
Maybe like my captain, the Musashi's captain
is trying to protect the ship all by herself.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:39.200
My captain, Thea, always makes snap
judgments and acts with determination.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.870
She's a wonderful captain.

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:45.170
If the captain becomes nervous,
all of the crew follow suit.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:49.170
That's why my captain always keeps
that anxiety bottled up inside,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.380
and carries it on her back.
That's what she told me.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.010
I'm not an outstanding captain like that.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.140
But you saved me, didn't you?

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.810
I'm grateful to you,
for the fact that I can be here like this.

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:08.900
That's right. I used to be run-run,
but now I want to do my best.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.420
Um, Captain, could I have a minute?

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:30.880
What is it?

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:34.050
I haven't been able to receive communication,

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.930
but I picked up a faint radio signal
coming from within the ship.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.720
- Isn't it a cell phone?
- I don't think it is.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.100
We need to confirm it, right?

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.600
- Lead the way.
- Yes, ma'am.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.600
Shiro—

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:49.020
Deputy-captain, you have the bridge.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:50.480
Y-Yes, ma'am.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.660
So you can tell with that?

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.570
I don't think you can pick up
a radio signal with that.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:02.660
This way!

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.240
SICK BAY

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:05.160
Here?

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.880
Oh my, a ghost.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.550
That's Ms. Minami...

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.980
Little one. Cute.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:36.480
That was great, Isoroku. You caught a rat!

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.740
Huh? The color is different...

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:39.740
Don't touch it.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.070
That's not a rat.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.240
<i>Communications are back online!</i>

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.410
<i>Radar is back! Now I can see everything.</i>

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.210
<i>I can hear everything around us well.</i>

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:51.620
Could it be...

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.630
It seems this thing was the cause.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:56.760
What is it?

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.920
A creature with a genetic makeup
only slightly different from a rat,

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:02.470
and it's infected with a weird virus.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.600
I detected that virus in
the Chief Artillery Officer's blood, too.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:07.810
Virus?

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:08.770
Aye...

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.900
It's possible this thing was the cause
of the Chief Artillery Officer

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.770
going berserk
and the electronics breaking down.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:15.900
Aye.

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.610
Okay then, if we investigate that,
we may be able to save everyone!

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.490
It is possible.

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:23.870
Isoroku, that was great! You deserve a medal!

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:26.160
I'll call you rear admiral from now on!

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:27.160
Grand.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.120
Grand Rear Admiral!

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:31.460
Wouldn't it be bad to promote
something to rear admiral?

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:33.960
More importantly,
we need to report to the school!

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.710
<i>Dead ahead, strange floating object.</i>

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:38.210
It's a mine!

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:51.640
Beautiful!

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.560
It's like we're on the clouds!

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:54.520
Amazing.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.360
But we're surrounded by mines.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:00.360
Is it okay to poke it?

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:03.610
The ones near us
are old contact detonation mines,

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:06.320
so unless I push one of its bumps,
it's no problem.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.870
I think we should detonate all of them.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.620
Until this mist clears up, we won't know
how many are around us.

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:17.710
And it'll be scary if one goes off,
the rest will follow in a chain reaction.

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.500
How troublesome!

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.880
I did a search of the area with sonar
while it was still night.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.090
What's the range?

00:13:26.090 --> 00:13:30.890
The goal is probably to block us,
so it's a relatively narrow range.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.980
The type of mines set out are unknown,
but considering the depth of the sea,

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:40.280
my guess is they're moored mines,
captor mines, and bottom mines.

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:42.400
What's a moored mine?

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:45.910
You know, the kind that's got
a wire attached, so if you bump into it,

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.660
BOOM!

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.290
We'll have to sweep the sea for them.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.080
What's the procedure for minesweeping?

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.830
Allow me to explain!

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:57.540
First, we connect
minesweeping tools to sweep wires

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:59.460
and drop deployers into the sea.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.920
As the ship keeps moving,
the deployers spread out left and right,

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.340
the submergers are pulled along by the stern,

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:12.720
and when a mine wires gets caught
on a sweep wire, it keeps moving

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.600
until a cutter snips right through it.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:22.280
As for the rest of the floating mines,
we'll use guns to shoot them and go BOOM!

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.860
That's my scene! Hurry up and let me shoot!

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:31.240
It's impossible to move the ship when
we're surrounded with mines like this.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:33.200
Tools. None.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.880
We don't have real minesweeping tools,
but we have to do what we can.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.420
It's dangerous to dispose of them
underwater with a human crew.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.630
Let's use a skipper.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:50.050
Certainly, it's small, so there's
small chance of it activating the mines

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.760
that detonate from sound,
water pressure, or magnetism.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.520
Safe.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.850
We'll give the skipper crew standard
and extra-safe equipment.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:00.860
Please don't be one of them, Captain.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:04.360
All right.

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.910
Huh? Mina, you didn't like the natto?

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.950
N-Nya, it's fine.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:14.700
- She fumbled her words.
- She did.

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:19.710
To be honest, Japanese food
doesn't really suit my taste.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.210
Oh, really? I'm sorry I didn't realize.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:25.920
Then why don't we make German food today?

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:27.590
No, no, no!

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.590
It's okay. I'm good at cooking German food.

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:34.260
Alright, we're going to have
a German food festival today!

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.100
Germans will be surprised at the taste.
Northern German food's King Eisbein!
Easy cooking with a pressure cooker.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.110
Repairs are done!

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:47.240
The surrounding mine situation is confirmed.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:48.820
Prepare to minesweep!

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.490
That's right. Minesweeping
is important for a ship's safe journey.

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:56.240
First clear the mines
that are in your line of sight.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:58.960
Alright! It's finally our turn. Let's go, Tama!

00:15:58.960 --> 00:15:59.660
Aye.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.630
Woohoo!

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.840
What a rush! This is how! Genghis Khan felt!

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:10.590
It's ready!

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:12.840
It's so cute!

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:15.220
Hey, let's give it a name!

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:18.270
It's a seal, so let's call it... Tama!

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:20.560
Aye?

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.360
That was too close!

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:28.360
Still, I wonder who set out those mines.
They're so dangerous.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:31.950
Mines placed in the past have traveled
through the ages to be revived now!

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:35.990
The mines were sucked into the Sargasso
and disappeared, only to reappear here!

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.410
It has to be a certain country's evil plot!

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:39.620
Evil plot?

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:45.130
I think the mines in this area were
set up by different nations to protect

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:49.380
their national interests and safeguard
their sea lanes in the early twentieth century.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.720
There's no romance in reality.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.260
It would be awful if we were in a war again!

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.930
To stop that from happening,
the Blue Mermaids

00:16:56.930 --> 00:17:00.480
were formed to transcend national
boundaries and protect the sea, right?

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:02.640
The Blue Mermaids' main mission

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.650
is to save lives and keep sea lanes safe
by doing things like minesweeping.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:08.900
Live on the sea...

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:10.400
Protect the sea...

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:12.030
- Sail...
- The sea.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.780
That's the Blue Mermaids!

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:19.950
<i>Just remember to be safe.</i>

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:21.450
Roger!

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:28.500
Deployed!

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:29.750
Begin minesweeping!

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.260
Roger! Full speed ahead!

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.720
Don't go that fast!

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:45.600
Ricchan, they're starting to come up.

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:48.360
Alright! We're going to keep—

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:55.280
- What was that?
- Report!

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:57.280
Underwater explosion dead ahead!

00:17:57.280 --> 00:17:58.950
The skipper got caught up in it!

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.410
<i>They're sending out a distress signal!</i>

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:03.540
<i>Two responses, it's the safety device.</i>

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:06.370
- I need to go help them!
- Again!

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.040
You intend to desert your post again, Captain?

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:11.500
B-But...

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.260
I'll go!

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.800
Captain, please help me!

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:17.800
O-Okay!

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:20.560
Deputy-Captain, will you take the bridge?

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:22.930
What? Uh, but...

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.140
Deputy-captain, you're in charge!

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:27.730
Safety of the crew and ship
are the first priority!

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:31.110
Ms. Marikouji, do a thorough check
to see if any mines were missed!

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:31.820
Roger!

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:40.330
This is wrong...

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:43.950
It's the captain's job to lead
from the ship at all times!

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:49.040
"Always on the deck!"
Isn't that how it's supposed to be?!

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:53.000
I was happy.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:58.720
Even though I do nothing but run away,
you accepted me, Ms. Misaki.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:04.270
I have no idea what an ideal captain is like.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.770
But I'm glad you're my captain, Ms. Misaki.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.270
Thank you, Rin.

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:20.660
Huh? What happened to me again?

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:23.540
We were minesweeping...

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:30.040
Oh yeah, I'm inside the safety device.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.670
Ricchan! Ri, where are you?!

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:39.880
Will someone come to save us?

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:43.430
I'm sure they will, right?

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:50.900
Kayo, are you okay?

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.900
Here, grab my hand.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:19:59.150
Captain.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:03.870
Kayo!

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:09.790
Ricchan! Thank goodness!

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.290
Rescue successful!

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:13.540
Alright!

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:27.060
Um, when you're talking
German dishes, this is a must.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:28.060
Eisbein!

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:32.810
That's a northern dish.
In my area, it's schweinshaxe...

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.570
In other words, we usually roast.

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:38.570
T-Then let's go to the next staple.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:39.690
Sauerkraft!

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:42.110
That's sauerkraut.

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:45.240
And this is just pickled cabbage.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:48.700
For the real thing,
lactic acid fermentations takes place.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:51.080
Next, cutlet!

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:54.330
- That's just breaded pork cutlet, right?
- Cutlets are German food?

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:56.880
Schnitzel!

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:00.880
It isn't cut this thick in my country.

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:04.680
Okay, this is the star performer!
If it's German food, then it's...

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.350
...hamburger!

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:09.100
Is that frikadeller?

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:11.100
It's food I don't often see in Germany.

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:12.440
What?!

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:16.650
That aside, this steamed potato
and eintopf look delicious!

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:21.030
Now if you just have wurst,
I have no complaints at sea!

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:22.740
Who made this?

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.490
We did...

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.580
I-I lost...

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:34.210
Are you okay?!

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:37.170
So hamburgers aren't German food then!

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:39.630
Mikan, this is delicious!

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:48.010
Episode 6: In a Mined Pinch!

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.980
Well, I have made a kind of antibody.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.280
I just hope it works.

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.990
"If you desire knowledge, you must act.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:36.410
The process of study should not end
until you practice it."

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:37.830
Stop, Ms. Minami!

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:40.080
Stop it!

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:42.420
If something happens, stop me.
